The header side of the pairing is the same field read from the
other end: a run block carries its own id on a line of its own,
and that is the line a trailer commit points back to.
We give it a name here, next to the trailer it pairs with, because
both the indexing below and the merging further down ask the same
question of a block---which run is this?---and neither should have
to know how the recorder spells the field.

<<commits functions>>=
def run_block_id(block):
    """Return the run id recorded in ``block``, or ``None``."""
    for line in block.split("\n"):
        if line.startswith(RUN_ID_FIELD):
            return line[len(RUN_ID_FIELD):].strip()
    return None
@

\subsection{Indexing the log by run id}
\label{sec:run-index}

A reader needs the trailer of a run before it can show that run,
and it cannot know which commit holds it without looking.
Reading every message once is therefore both simplest and
cheapest: one [[git log]] call for the whole map, instead of one
call per commit to hunt for its partner.
As in [[get_commits]], an empty history makes [[git log]] exit
non-zero; that is no trailers rather than an error, so the call opts
out of [[check]] (\cref{gitrepo}).
The walk starts from the recording branch rather than from
[[HEAD]], for the reason \cref{sec:run-visibility} spells out for
[[get_commits]]: the two must read the same history, or a run
could be listed whose trailer this map does not hold.

The same pass answers the opposite question for free.
Refs land on trailer commits (\cref{sec:trailer-refs}), and
resolving such a ref means going from a trailer commit \emph{back}
to the header that carries its run's record---the same pairing by
run id, read in the other direction.

The pass also keeps every message it reads, keyed by commit.
Before it did, the [[list]] command asked Git again for each
commit's message---one [[git log -1]] per run, right after this
scan had read them all.  Each Git call costs a fork and an exec
whatever it does, so a semester's log paid hundreds of forks for
text that was already in memory here.  Keeping the messages makes
a listing cost the same three or four Git calls however long the
log grows.

We therefore come away with three maps, bundled as a named tuple
so callers can pick their slice by name: run id to trailer text,
trailer commit to the header commit of the same run, and every
commit to its message.

<<commits constants>>=
RunScan = collections.namedtuple(
    "RunScan",
    ["trailers", "records", "messages"],
)
<<commits functions>>=
def scan_run_commits(repo):
    """Index the log by run id in a single pass.

    Returns a `RunScan` named tuple.  `trailers` maps a run id
    to the trailer text recorded for it; `records` maps the hash
    of a trailer commit to the header commit that carries the same
    run's record; `messages` maps every commit hash on the
    recording branch to its full commit message.
    """
    result = repo.git(
        "log", "-z", "--format=%H%n%B",
        repo.recording_branch(), check=False,
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return RunScan({}, {}, {})

    trailers = {}
    headers = {}
    trailer_runs = {}
    messages = {}
    for commit_hash, message in iter_log_commits(result.stdout):
        messages[commit_hash] = message
        parsed = parse_trailer_message(message)
        if parsed is None:
            <<note which commit holds each header block>>
            continue
        run_id, trailer = parsed
        trailers[run_id] = trailer
        trailer_runs[commit_hash] = run_id
    <<pair each trailer commit with its header commit>>
    return RunScan(trailers, records, messages)
@

A header commit usually holds one run block, but not always: older
repositories grouped consecutive runs of unchanged code into one
message, and so does ProgSnap2 reconstruction
(\cref{sec:block-sep}).
Splitting first means each of those runs is indexed on the commit
that really carries it, rather than only the first one being
found.

<<note which commit holds each header block>>=
for block in message.split(BLOCK_SEP):
    run_id = run_block_id(block)
    if run_id:
        headers[run_id] = commit_hash
@

The two halves are joined at the end rather than as they are met,
because [[git log]] hands us the trailer commit first: it is the
younger of the pair, and its header is still several messages
ahead in the walk.
A trailer whose header never turns up is left out of the map, so
that the caller can tell \enquote{this run's header is over there}
from \enquote{this trailer names a run this history does not
have}, and fall back accordingly.

<<pair each trailer commit with its header commit>>=
records = {
    commit_hash: headers[run_id]
    for commit_hash, run_id in trailer_runs.items()
    if run_id in headers
}
@

Most maps have callers that want only that slice, and asking for
one should not make the others' names appear at the call site.
The two accessors below are what most of the program uses;
[[scan_run_commits]] is the shared walk behind them.  A caller
that needs several slices at once---the [[list]] command reads
both trailers and messages---calls the walk itself, so the log is
still read only once.

<<commits functions>>=
def get_run_trailers(repo):
    """Map run ids to the trailer text recorded for them."""
    return scan_run_commits(repo).trailers
<<commits functions>>=
def get_record_commits(repo):
    """Map trailer commits to the header commit of their run."""
    return scan_run_commits(repo).records
@

Asking Git for NUL-separated records ([[-z]]) is what makes the
messages separable at all: they contain blank lines, dashes, and
whole captured terminal sessions, so no textual delimiter is
safe.
A NUL is safe only because Git never puts one in a commit
message---but captured program output can contain one, and a
future writer might let it through, so we do not trust the
separator alone.
Each record starts with the commit hash on its own line, and a
fragment that does not is a piece of the previous message that a
stray NUL split off; we glue it back rather than reporting half a
run.
That leading line is worth keeping rather than dropping: it is the
only place the walk learns \emph{which} commit a message came
from, and the index above is built out of exactly those pairs.

<<commits functions>>=
def iter_log_commits(output):
    """Yield ``(hash, message)`` pairs from ``git log``.

    Reads the output of ``git log -z --format=%H%n%B``.
    """
    records = []
    for fragment in output.split("\0"):
        if not fragment:
            continue
        if records and not _starts_with_commit_hash(fragment):
            records[-1] += "\0" + fragment
        else:
            records.append(fragment)

    for record in records:
        commit_hash, _, message = record.partition("\n")
        yield commit_hash.strip(), message.rstrip("\n")
@

<<commits functions>>=
def _starts_with_commit_hash(fragment):
    """True if ``fragment`` begins with a hash on its own line."""
    line = fragment.partition("\n")[0]
    return len(line) in COMMIT_HASH_LENGTHS and all(
        character in "0123456789abcdef"
        for character in line
    )
<<commits constants>>=
COMMIT_HASH_LENGTHS = (40, 64)
@

Both hash lengths are listed because a repository can use SHA-1 or
SHA-256 object names, and which one a student's Git picks is
configuration we do not control.

Let's verify that a NUL inside a commit message does not split a
record in two:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_iter_log_commits_survives_embedded_nul():
    from learnlog.commits import iter_log_commits
    output = (
        "a" * 40 + "\nRun: hello.py\nstdout: be\0fore\n\0"
        + "b" * 40 + "\nRun: other.py\n\0"
    )
    commits = list(iter_log_commits(output))
    assert len(commits) == 2
    assert commits[0][0] == "a" * 40
    assert "stdout: be\0fore" in commits[0][1]
    assert commits[1] == ("b" * 40, "Run: other.py")
@

\subsection{Merging a trailer into its block}

Merging appends the trailer to the block whose run id it names.
A block that already ends in a trailer is left alone: messages
from older repositories and from ProgSnap2 reconstruction are
complete in themselves, and appending to them would duplicate the
results.

<<commits functions>>=
def merge_run_trailers(message, trailers):
    """Attach recorded trailers to the run blocks in ``message``.

    ``trailers`` is the map from :func:`get_run_trailers`.
    Blocks with no recorded trailer---an interrupted run, or an
    already complete block---are returned unchanged.
    """
    if not message or not trailers:
        return message

    blocks = []
    for block in message.split(BLOCK_SEP):
        trailer = trailers.get(run_block_id(block))
        if trailer and END_TIME_FIELD not in block:
            block = block.rstrip("\n") + "\n" + trailer
        blocks.append(block)
    return BLOCK_SEP.join(blocks)
<<commits constants>>=
END_TIME_FIELD = "End-Time:"
@

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_merge_run_trailers_completes_block():
    from learnlog.commits import merge_run_trailers
    header = (
        "Run: hello.py (none)\n"
        "\n"
        "Script: hello.py\n"
        "Arguments: (none)\n"
        "Start-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:00\n"
        "Run-Id: abc123"
    )
    trailers = {
        "abc123": "End-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:05\nExit-Code: 0",
    }
    merged = merge_run_trailers(header, trailers)
    assert merged.startswith(header)
    assert "Exit-Code: 0" in merged

def test_merge_run_trailers_leaves_unfinished_run():
    """A run killed before finalising keeps its bare header."""
    from learnlog.commits import merge_run_trailers
    header = (
        "Run: hello.py (none)\n\nRun-Id: killed"
