The two overlaps get a test each, because they fail differently
and only one of the two failures announces itself.
Both build the history from two [[LearnlogRepo]] instances, which
is how an interleaved pair of processes writes it
(\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}): each instance carries its own
run id, so the commits land in the order the calls are made.

An inner run finishing first leaves a trailer commit immediately
before the outer run's trailer, so a tag on the newest commit used
to resolve to the inner run's trailer---a commit no listing has.
The assertion checks both halves of what the callers need---the
run the tag names, and that the answer is a commit [[get_commits]]
lists, since that list is what [[play --from]] looks the answer up
in and what it fails on with \enquote{not in the commit list}.

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_resolve_record_commit_lifo_overlap(workdir):
    """A tag on the newest trailer names the run it completes."""
    from learnlog.commits import (
        get_commits,
        resolve_record_commit,
    )
    outer = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    inner = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    t0 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)

    outer.begin_run("outer.py", [], t0)
    outer_header = outer.git(
        "rev-parse", "HEAD",
    ).stdout.strip()
    inner.begin_run("inner.py", [], t0)
    inner.finalize_run(
        io_log="", exception_info=None,
        end_time=t0 + datetime.timedelta(seconds=1),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    outer.finalize_run(
        io_log="", exception_info=None,
        end_time=t0 + datetime.timedelta(seconds=2),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    newest = outer.git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()

    resolved = resolve_record_commit(outer, newest)

    assert resolved == outer_header
    assert resolved in [c["hash"] for c in get_commits(outer)]
@

Finishing in the order the runs started puts a \emph{header}
immediately before a trailer instead, so the topological answer is
a commit every listing contains---and it belongs to the wrong run.
Nothing reports an error in this case: the student tags one run
and playback shows them another.
The scripts are named apart so the assertion can say which run was
found rather than only which hash.

