\section{Elapsed time between runs}

Teachers often care not only about \emph{what} a student ran, but also
about how long they paused before the next attempt.
The playback viewer only needs a lightweight approximation, so we use the
commit author date that [[begin_run]] records at run start.

That approximation is good enough for a quick \enquote{instant retry vs.
deliberate pause} signal, but it is still slightly noisy.
Interrupted runs may never record an end time, and playback works from Git
commit summaries rather than full run blocks.
The helper here therefore measures the interval from the previous displayed
student commit to the current one, while the ProgSnap2 analyser computes the
more precise end-to-start interval from the run data itself.

\subsection{Parsing ISO timestamps defensively}
\label{sec:parse-iso}

The run logs and Git commit dates both use ISO timestamps.
We treat empty or malformed strings as missing data rather than raising, so
the viewer can simply omit the elapsed-time line when the underlying data is
incomplete.

<<commits functions>>=
def _parse_iso(value):
    """Return ``datetime.fromisoformat(value)`` or ``None``.

    Empty or invalid timestamps are treated as missing.
    """
    if not value:
        return None
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(value)
    except ValueError:
        return None
@

Let's verify that valid timestamps parse and invalid ones quietly disappear:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_parse_iso_valid_and_invalid():
    from learnlog.commits import _parse_iso
    assert _parse_iso("2026-01-01T10:00:00") == \
        datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
    assert _parse_iso("") is None
    assert _parse_iso("not-a-date") is None
@

A timestamp is normalised where it is parsed, not where it is compared,
so that every reader of the log gets the same answer without each
deciding for itself.
The recorder writes [[datetime.now().isoformat()]], a naive local time,
and every comparison the readers make---the listing's windows
(\cref{sec:list-command}), the sessions and buckets of the metrics
(\cref{metrics})---is between such values.
A history that went through a ProgSnap2 round trip or another machine
may carry an offset, so an aware value is moved to local time and its
offset dropped, while a naive one is trusted.
[[utcnow]] never appears, because the student's day boundaries are
local ones.

<<commits functions>>=
def local_naive(value):
    """Return ``value`` as a naive local datetime, or ``None``."""
    if value is None:
        return None
    if value.tzinfo is not None:
        return value.astimezone().replace(tzinfo=None)
    return value
@

Let's verify that a naive value passes through untouched, that
[[None]] stays [[None]], and that an aware value loses its offset:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_local_naive_keeps_naive_and_strips_offset():
    from learnlog.commits import local_naive
    naive = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0)
    assert local_naive(naive) is naive
    assert local_naive(None) is None
    aware = datetime.datetime(
        2026, 1, 1, 10, 0, tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc,
    )
    assert local_naive(aware).tzinfo is None
@


\subsection{Formatting elapsed time for humans}

Playback and analysis both need the same human-readable rendering.
We keep the format compact so it fits naturally in a one-line status field:
seconds for short retries, minutes and seconds for ordinary pauses, and hours
and minutes for longer breaks.

Negative intervals can happen if clocks skew or imported data is inconsistent.
Rather than showing a confusing negative duration, we clamp anything below one
second to [[< 1 s]].

<<commits functions>>=
def format_elapsed(seconds):
    """Return a compact human-readable elapsed time.

    Returns ``""`` for ``None``.  Values below one second,
    including negative ones, are rendered as ``"< 1 s"``.
    """
    if seconds is None:
        return ""

    if seconds < 1:
        return "< 1 s"

    total = int(seconds)
    if total < 60:
        return f"{total} s"

    if total < 3600:
        minutes, secs = divmod(total, 60)
        if secs == 0:
            return f"{minutes} min"
        return f"{minutes} min {secs} s"

    hours = total // 3600
    minutes = (total % 3600) // 60
    return f"{hours} h {minutes} min"
@

We can pin down the display choices with a few representative cases:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_format_elapsed_human_readable():
    from learnlog.commits import format_elapsed
    assert format_elapsed(None) == ""
    assert format_elapsed(-5) == "< 1 s"
    assert format_elapsed(0.2) == "< 1 s"
    assert format_elapsed(5) == "5 s"
    assert format_elapsed(120) == "2 min"
    assert format_elapsed(135) == "2 min 15 s"
    assert format_elapsed(3780) == "1 h 3 min"
@


\subsection{Recognising learnlog's own commits}

A [[Run: learnlog ...]] block can mean three things to whoever reads
the log, and the three want three different treatments.
[[learnlog play]], [[learnlog list]] and [[learnlog metrics]] are the
student stepping back through their own history, and the recorder
logs exactly those three subcommands because that reflection is itself
evidence of learning (\cref{learnlog}).
The record is written in full and reaches every researcher path
unchanged---the export (\cref{progsnap2}) and the metrics
(\cref{metrics}) both see it---but a student who reads their log ten
times in an evening has ten records of reading for one of writing, and
a listing that buries the writing under the reading defeats the
purpose of keeping either.
So the two reading views, [[list]] and [[play]], fold review runs away
until [[--all]] asks for them.
[[learnlog checkout]] is the one learnlog run that changes the files
(\cref{sec:checkout}); going back is work, so it is shown like any
run of the student's own.
Everything else learnlog leaves behind is plumbing: the [[pip]] install
that [[learnlog init]] performs, and---in repositories written before
the recorder narrowed to review runs---records of the remaining
subcommands, which used to log themselves as well.
That kind is noise in every view, so [[--all]] is the only way to see
it.

Five predicates therefore share one text-matching core, and each view
picks the one that matches its question.
[[is_learnlog_command]] asks \enquote{is this learnlog at all}, which
is what the elapsed indicator needs; [[is_review_command]] picks out
the reflection runs and [[is_checkout_command]] the checkouts;
[[is_internal_command]] is the plumbing remainder, and
[[is_hidden_command]] is the union the reading views filter
by---everything of learnlog's except a checkout.

All of them read the command line rather than the commit, so the same
test serves a commit subject (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}) and a parsed
run block (\cref{progsnap2}).

Installing learnlog counts as learnlog activity too.
[[learnlog init]] pip-installs the package into the project venv, and
that [[pip]] run is captured like any other; it is setup, not
development, so it is hidden by the same flag.
We match only [[pip]] commands that mention learnlog, so a student
installing their own dependencies still sees those runs.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_learnlog_command(command):
    """Return true if ``command`` is learnlog's own activity.

    Covers ``learnlog`` itself and the ``pip`` invocation that
    installs it during ``learnlog init``.
    """
    parts = command.split()
    if not parts:
        return False
    if parts[0] == "learnlog":
        return True
    return parts[0] in INSTALLER_COMMANDS and any(
        "learnlog" in argument for argument in parts[1:]
    )
<<commits constants>>=
INSTALLER_COMMANDS = ("pip", "pip3")
@

A commit records learnlog activity when either half of a run record
does: the header names the command, and the trailer repeats it, so
whichever commit we are looking at answers the question.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_learnlog_commit(commit):
    """Return true if ``commit`` records a learnlog CLI run."""
    subject = commit.get("subject", "")
    for prefix in (RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX, TRAILER_PREFIX):
        if subject.startswith(prefix):
            return is_learnlog_command(
                subject[len(prefix):]
            )
    return False
<<commits constants>>=
RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX = "Run: "
@

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_is_learnlog_commit():
    from learnlog.commits import is_learnlog_commit
    assert is_learnlog_commit({"subject": "Run: learnlog play"})
    assert is_learnlog_commit(
        {"subject": "Run-End: learnlog play"}
    )
    assert is_learnlog_commit(
        {"subject": "Run: pip install learnlog"}
    )
    assert not is_learnlog_commit({"subject": "Run: hello.py"})
    assert not is_learnlog_commit(
        {"subject": "Run: pip install requests"}
    )
    assert not is_learnlog_commit(
        {"subject": "learnlog: initial commit"}
    )
@

A review run is recognised by its subcommand, which makes the list of
review subcommands a piece of vocabulary that the recorder and this
reader must agree on: the recorder consults it to decide whether to
log an invocation at all (\cref{learnlog}), and disagreement would
mean runs that are recorded and then hidden, or filtered and never
written.
So [[REVIEW_SUBCOMMANDS]] is not defined here: it comes from
\cref{gitrepo} through the import above, exactly like
[[RUN_ID_FIELD]] and [[TRAILER_PREFIX]]---one definition that both
halves read is what makes disagreement impossible rather than merely
unlikely (\cref{sec:review-subcommands}).

Only the first two tokens decide.
Anything after them is options and arguments ([[learnlog list --all]]
is still a review run), and a bare [[learnlog]] with no subcommand is
not one---the recorder does not log it either.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_review_command(command):
    """Return true if ``command`` is a learnlog review run.

    Review runs are ``learnlog play``, ``learnlog list`` and
    ``learnlog metrics``: the subcommands with which a student
    looks back at their own history.
    """
    parts = command.split()
    return (
        len(parts) > 1
        and parts[0] == "learnlog"
        and parts[1] in REVIEW_SUBCOMMANDS
    )
@

A checkout is the student's too, and it is the one learnlog run that
the recorder does not write.
[[learnlog checkout]] restores the files of an earlier run and
records itself first, so that the files it replaces are in the log
before they go (\cref{sec:checkout}).
It is not a review---it changes the code rather than reads it, and
the exporter gives it an event type of its own for that reason
(\cref{sec:checkout-events})---so it gets a predicate of its own
rather than a place on the review list.
The subcommand's name is defined here rather than in \cref{gitrepo}
because the recorder never reads it: the two parties that have to
agree are the command that writes the record and the readers below,
and the command imports the name from here.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_checkout_command(command):
    """Return true if ``command`` is a ``learnlog checkout`` run."""
    parts = command.split()
    return (
        len(parts) > 1
        and parts[0] == "learnlog"
        and parts[1] == CHECKOUT_SUBCOMMAND
    )
<<commits constants>>=
CHECKOUT_SUBCOMMAND = "checkout"
@

The internal test is the difference: learnlog's own activity minus
the part of it that belongs to the student, the reviews and the
checkouts.
Writing it as a difference rather than as a second list of subcommands
means a subcommand can never be both, and that a repository recorded by
an older version---which logged subcommands this list has never heard
of---still classifies every one of them as plumbing.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_internal_command(command):
    """Return true if ``command`` is learnlog's own plumbing.

    Learnlog activity that is neither a review run nor a
    checkout: the ``pip`` install performed by ``learnlog
    init``, and records of other subcommands left by versions
    that logged them.
    """
    return (
        is_learnlog_command(command)
        and not is_review_command(command)
        and not is_checkout_command(command)
    )
@

The hidden test is the other difference: learnlog's own activity minus
the checkouts.
It is spelled as a difference for the same reason---a checkout can
never be hidden by accident, whatever the recorder of a future version
adds to its list---and it is the widest of the filters because a
reading view has exactly one question, \enquote{is this the student's
work}, to which a review and the plumbing both answer no.
[[is_internal_command]] remains the narrower question, and
\cref{sec:filter-learnlog} says which record it is still asked about.

<<commits functions>>=
def is_hidden_command(command):
    """Return true if a reading view hides ``command`` by default.

    Learnlog's own activity minus the checkouts: the review
    runs and the plumbing alike.
    """
    return (
        is_learnlog_command(command)
        and not is_checkout_command(command)
    )
@

