\section{Listing commits}

We list commits in chronological order (oldest first) because
that matches the natural playback direction: the student starts
writing code and we watch their progress forward in time.

A repository with no commits at all---a freshly initialised one, or a
bundle that turned out to be empty---makes [[git log]] exit non-zero.
That is an empty history rather than a broken one, so this call opts out
of [[check]] and reports it as an empty list.

\label{sec:run-visibility}
The listing walks the recording branch by name rather than
[[HEAD]], because the two are no longer the same thing.
Runs are committed to the branch wherever [[HEAD]] happens to
point (\cref{sec:recording-branch}); a student who detaches
[[HEAD]] with [[learnlog git checkout]] to look at an older
version keeps recording runs, and a reader that walked [[HEAD]]
would silently stop showing them---the newest runs would be the
missing ones, with nothing to say so.
Asking [[recording_branch]] makes the reader and the recorder
agree on which history the log is, whatever the student does with
[[HEAD]].

Trailer commits are left out here, which is what makes every
caller---listing, playback, and export---see one entry per run
without knowing about the two-commit protocol.
Dropping them loses nothing: a trailer carries no code state and
no new command, and its contents reach the reader through
[[merge_run_trailers]] instead.

<<commits functions>>=
def get_commits(repo):
    """Return a list of commits in chronological order.

    Each entry is a dict with ``hash``, ``subject``, and
    ``date`` keys.  An empty history yields an empty list.
    Trailer commits are excluded: they complete the record of a
    run that is already in the list.
    """
    result = repo.git(
        "log", "--format=%H\t%s\t%aI", "--reverse",
        repo.recording_branch(), check=False,
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return []
    commits = []
    for line in result.stdout.strip().split("\n"):
        if not line:
            continue
        parts = line.split("\t", 2)
        if len(parts) == 3 and \
                not parts[1].startswith(TRAILER_PREFIX):
            commits.append({
                "hash": parts[0],
                "subject": parts[1],
                "date": parts[2],
            })
    return commits
@

