\section{Commit details}

For a single commit we use [[git show]], which produces the
commit header, the full message (containing the captured I/O),
and the unified diff (showing the code that changed)---all in a
single subprocess call.

A teacher reviewing student work typically wants to see
\emph{what changed} before reading the run metadata (script
name, arguments, captured I/O).
The default [[git show]] order is header, message, diff; we
rearrange to header, diff, message so that code changes appear
first.


\subsection{Parsing [[git show]] sections}

The [[git show]] output always starts with metadata lines
(commit hash, author, date) followed by a blank line, then the
indented commit message, then the diff.
Both reordering and filtering need to locate these three sections,
so we extract the boundary-finding into a single function.

The header ends at the first blank line.
The diff starts at the first [[diff --git]] line; when no diff is
present---for example, the initial commit that only records a run
without any file changes---[[diff_start]] is [[None]].

<<commits functions>>=
def parse_show_sections(lines):
    """Find section boundaries in ``git show`` output.

    Returns ``(header_end, diff_start)`` where
    ``header_end`` is the index of the first blank line and
    ``diff_start`` is the index of the first ``diff --git``
    line, or ``None`` if no diff is present.
    """
    header_end = 0
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line == "":
            header_end = i
            break

    diff_start = None
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line.startswith("diff --git "):
            diff_start = i
            break

    return header_end, diff_start
@


\subsection{Splitting the three sections apart}
\label{sec:split-commit-detail}

Boundaries alone are not what most callers want; they want the three
pieces, with the message as text rather than as indented lines.
Git indents the message by four spaces in [[git show]] output, so
every reader of the message begins by undoing that---and a reader
that forgets is not obviously broken, it merely reports four spaces
of leading whitespace on every line of a transcript that is supposed
to be exact.
Undoing it in one place is what keeps that mistake from being
available to make.

The diff comes back as an empty list rather than as [[None]] when
the commit has none---which is the initial commit, recording a run
without changing a file---so a caller can concatenate the three
parts without first asking whether the third exists.

<<commits functions>>=
def split_commit_detail(detail):
    """Split ``git show`` output into its three parts.

    Returns `(header_lines, message, diff_lines)`, where
    `message` is the commit message with git's four-space
    indent removed, and `diff_lines` is empty when the commit
    has no diff.
    """
    lines = detail.split("\n")
    header_end, diff_start = parse_show_sections(lines)
    end = diff_start if diff_start is not None else len(lines)

    message = "\n".join(
        line[4:] if line.startswith("    ") else line
        for line in lines[header_end + 1:end]
    )
    return lines[:header_end + 1], message, lines[end:]
@

Let's verify both shapes---a commit whose diff has to be found, and
one that has none:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_split_commit_detail_with_diff():
    from learnlog.commits import split_commit_detail
    detail = (
        "commit abc123\n"
        "Author: Test <test@test>\n"
        "\n"
        "    Run: hello.py\n"
        "\n"
        "    Exit-Code: 0\n"
