\subsection{Reordering commit detail}

Given the section boundaries, rearranging is a matter of slicing the
lines into header, message, and diff, then reassembling them in the
order that puts code changes first.
When no diff is present (the initial commit), we leave the output
unchanged.

This is the one reader that slices the lines itself instead of
calling [[split_commit_detail]], and the reason is what that function
does for everyone else: it un-indents the message.
Reordering hands the message straight back to a viewer as part of
[[git show]] output, where it is supposed to be indented, so
stripping four spaces only to put them back would be work whose only
possible effect is to get it wrong.

<<commits functions>>=
def reorder_commit_detail(detail):
    """Reorder ``git show`` output: header, diff, message.

    Moves the unified diff before the commit message so that
    code changes are visible first in the viewer.
    """
    lines = detail.split("\n")
    header_end, diff_start = parse_show_sections(lines)

    if diff_start is None:
        return detail

    header = lines[:header_end + 1]
    message = lines[header_end + 1:diff_start]
    diff = lines[diff_start:]

    return "\n".join(header + diff + [""] + message)
@


\section{Filtering runs out of a message}
\label{sec:filter-learnlog}

Three questions are asked of a [[Run: learnlog ...]] block---is it
plumbing, is it anything of learnlog's but a checkout, or is nothing
hidden at all---and the same surgery answers all of them: split the
message on the block separator, drop the blocks whose header names a
command the question accepts, and join what is left.
So the filter takes the question as a parameter.
The block level is where every one of them has to be asked, because a
message can carry several blocks (\cref{sec:block-sep}) and a mixed
message must keep its student runs.
A filter that hides nothing is spelled as the absence of a filter,
[[None]], so that [[--all]] costs its callers no branch of their own.

<<commits functions>>=
def filter_runs(message, hide):
    """Remove the run blocks ``hide`` names from a message.

    ``hide`` is a predicate on the command a block's ``Run:``
    header names, or ``None`` to keep every block.  Splits on
    the ``---`` block separator and drops the blocks ``hide``
    accepts.  Returns the filtered message, which may be empty
    if every block was hidden.
    """
    if hide is None:
        return message
    kept = [
        block for block in message.split(BLOCK_SEP)
        if not _block_hidden(block, hide)
    ]
    return BLOCK_SEP.join(kept)
@

A block's first line is its [[Run:]] header, which carries the same
command string as the commit subject---so the block-level and the
commit-level tests ask the predicate the same question about the same
text.
A block with no such header---the initial commit's message, or a
fragment---names no command, and no predicate is asked about it.

<<commits functions>>=
def _block_hidden(block, hide):
    """True if ``hide`` accepts the command ``block`` records."""
    first_line = block.lstrip().split("\n", 1)[0]
    if not first_line.startswith(RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX):
        return False
    return hide(first_line[len(RUN_SUBJECT_PREFIX):])
@

The plumbing filter is the instance every reader of [[git show]] output
applies unless told otherwise (\cref{sec:filter-show}), and the one
the ProgSnap2 round trip is held to (\cref{progsnap2}): what an import
has to preserve is the record, and the record is what this filter
leaves standing.

<<commits functions>>=
def filter_internal_runs(message):
    """Remove learnlog's plumbing blocks from a message.

    Review runs (``learnlog play``, ``list`` and ``metrics``)
    and checkouts are kept, as are the student's own programs.
    """
    return filter_runs(message, is_internal_command)
@

Which of the wider filters a reading view applies is decided once per
commit, and in one place.
[[--all]] hides nothing, and neither does a tag.
[[learnlog tag]] names whatever ran last (\cref{sec:tag}), which is
often the [[learnlog list]] the student consulted before choosing the
name, and a view that hid the record they chose to name would lose
the milestone marker it exists to show---the [[(tag: lab1)]] in the
listing, and the place [[learnlog play lab1]] starts.
The tag is the student's mark, not learnlog's noise, whatever the
record holds: a tag on the [[pip]] install that [[learnlog init]]
performed shows that install, rather than a marker with nothing
under it.
Every other record hides everything of learnlog's but a checkout.

<<commits functions>>=
def hidden_runs(*, show_all=False, tagged=False):
    """The block filter a reading view applies to one commit.

    ``None`` hides nothing, which is what ``--all`` asks for and
    what a tagged record gets; every other record hides all of
    learnlog's own runs except a checkout.
    """
    if show_all or tagged:
        return None
    return is_hidden_command
@

The exporter (\cref{progsnap2}) asks the same questions about a run it
has already parsed into fields, where the command is split in two.
Rebuilding the command line from those fields is the only part that
differs from the block-level and commit-level tests, so it is the only
part written once and shared by all four run-level predicates:

<<commits functions>>=
def _run_command(run):
    """Rebuild the command line of a parsed run block."""
    script = run.get("script") or ""
    arguments = run.get("arguments") or ""
    return f"{script} {arguments}"
@

<<commits functions>>=
def is_learnlog_run(run):
    """True if a parsed run block records learnlog's activity."""
    return is_learnlog_command(_run_command(run))
@

<<commits functions>>=
def is_review_run(run):
    """True if a parsed run block records a review run."""
    return is_review_command(_run_command(run))
@

<<commits functions>>=
def is_checkout_run(run):
    """True if a parsed run block records a checkout."""
    return is_checkout_command(_run_command(run))
@

<<commits functions>>=
def is_internal_run(run):
    """True if a parsed run block records learnlog plumbing."""
    return is_internal_command(_run_command(run))
@

A parsed block with no command at all---a fragment, or a block the
parser could make nothing of---is nobody's activity, so every one of
the three answers false rather than guessing:

