\section{Aggregating outcomes across a commit}
\label{sec:commit-outcomes}

A single commit can contain several run blocks when the student
ran unchanged code multiple times.  The [[list]] filter only
needs to know \emph{which} outcomes appear in a commit, not the
order or count---it intersects the commit's outcome set with the
set of outcomes the user asked to see and includes the commit if
the intersection is non-empty.

Returning a set rather than a list captures this use case
directly: a commit where the student got code working on one
attempt and broke it on a re-run appears under both
[[--success]] (the working attempt is still there) and
[[--failure]] (the breakage is there too).  This is essential
for the recovery workflow---the last [[--success]] line must
still point at such a commit, because that is what the student
would check out.

<<commits functions>>=
def commit_outcomes(message):
    """Return the set of outcomes found in a commit message.

    Splits ``message`` on ``BLOCK_SEP`` and classifies each run
    block.  The returned set contains any of ``"success"``,
    ``"failure"``, and ``"unknown"``.  Non-run blocks (e.g. the
    initial commit's subject line) are ignored.
    """
    outcomes = set()
    for block in message.split(BLOCK_SEP):
        if not block.strip().startswith("Run:"):
            continue
        outcomes.add(classify_run_block(block))
    return outcomes
@

Let's verify that a commit with both a successful and a failed
run reports both outcomes:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_commit_outcomes_mixed():
    from learnlog.commits import commit_outcomes
    msg = (
        "Run: hello.py\n\nScript: hello.py\n"
        "Exit-Code: 0\n--- exception ---\nNone"
        "\n\n---\n\n"
        "Run: hello.py\n\nScript: hello.py\n"
        "Exit-Code: 1\n--- exception ---\nNone"
    )
    assert commit_outcomes(msg) == {"success", "failure"}
@

A message with no run blocks (for instance, the initial
[[learnlog: initial commit]] or a message where all runs were
filtered out) yields an empty set so the [[list]] filter
silently skips it:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_commit_outcomes_no_runs():
    from learnlog.commits import commit_outcomes
    assert commit_outcomes("learnlog: initial commit") == set()
    assert commit_outcomes("") == set()
@


\section{Parsing a run block in full}
\label{sec:parse-run-block}

[[classify_run_block]] above reads a block for a single bit of
information.  Everything else that meets a run block wants all of
it: the ProgSnap2 exporter turns each field into a column of the
event table (\cref{progsnap2}), the metrics module reads exit codes
and tracebacks to tell a syntax error from a runtime one
(\cref{metrics}), and showing a student their own run means
rendering the same fields a third time.

The full parser began life in the exporter, back when the exporter
was the only reader that needed it.  That placement contradicts the
separation this chapter opens with: reading a recorded run is this
module's job, and a reader that has to import the exporter to do it
drags the whole ProgSnap2 chapter---zip files, temporary Git
repositories, \LaTeX{} generation---behind a question about one
commit message.  So the parser lives here, beside the classifier
that answers the narrower question, and the exporter imports it like
every other reader.

Keeping both is not duplication, because the two disagree, usefully,
about what a block has to look like.  The classifier treats any
line below an [[--- exception ---]] marker as exception text, so a
truncated or hand-assembled block still yields a verdict; the parser
below cuts the two sections exactly where [[format_run_trailer]]
joined them, because its promise is that what the recorder wrote
comes back out unchanged.  Neither tolerance would serve both jobs:
loosening the parser would break that promise, and tightening the
classifier would withhold a verdict from blocks a teacher can still
read perfectly well.

[[parse_run_summary]] above extracts just enough for the [[list]]
command: command string, start time, and run count.
The readers named above need every field of every run block---exit
code, end time, exception type, I/O---which is what this parser
supplies.

A run block has two parts: a \emph{header} written by
[[begin_run]] (always present) and a \emph{trailer} written by
[[finalize_run]] (missing if the program was killed by SIGKILL).
The header contains [[Run:]], [[Script:]], [[Arguments:]], and
[[Start-Time:]]; the trailer contains [[End-Time:]],
[[Exit-Code:]], [[--- I/O ---]], and [[--- exception ---]].

We parse each block into a flat dict with string values, keeping
the raw text for I/O and exception sections.  Missing trailer
fields are represented as [[None]]---this lets callers distinguish
\enquote{program crashed} from \enquote{exit code was zero}.

A review run's trailer carries a further section, the [[Viewed:]]
lines naming the commits the student looked at.
Reading it is [[parse_view_trace]]'s job (\cref{sec:view-trace}); we
call it here so that every consumer of a parsed block sees the trace
as just another field, and so that ordinary runs---which have no such
lines---come back with an empty list rather than a missing key.

<<commits functions>>=
def parse_run_block(block_text):
    """Parse a single run block into a dict.

    Returns a dict with keys ``script``, ``arguments``,
    ``start_time``, ``end_time``, ``exit_code``,
    ``io_log``, ``exception_info``, and ``viewed``.
    Missing trailer fields are ``None``; ``viewed`` is the
    view trace, empty for everything but a review run.
    """
    result = {
        "script": None, "arguments": None,
        "start_time": None, "end_time": None,
        "exit_code": None, "io_log": None,
        "exception_info": None,
        "viewed": parse_view_trace(block_text),
    }

    <<parse key-value fields>>
    <<parse trailer fields>>

    return result
@

Both header and trailer use simple [[Key: value]] lines, so
we parse them in a single pass.
We skip the first [[Run:]] line because it duplicates the
[[Script:]] and [[Arguments:]] fields in a less structured form.

<<parse key-value fields>>=
for line in block_text.split("\n"):
    if line.startswith("Script:"):
        result["script"] = line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
    elif line.startswith("Arguments:"):
        result["arguments"] = (
            line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
        )
    elif line.startswith("Start-Time:"):
        result["start_time"] = (
            line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
        )
    elif line.startswith("End-Time:"):
        result["end_time"] = (
            line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
        )
    elif line.startswith("Exit-Code:"):
        result["exit_code"] = (
            line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
        )
@

The I/O and exception sections are delimited by the markers
[[format_run_trailer]] writes (\cref{sec:io-section}), and the I/O
section is read back by cutting exactly at them.
The parser this replaced took the text between the markers and
[[strip]]ped it, which is the natural thing to write and wrong for
this format: a captured log is a transcript, so spaces at the start of
its first line and at the end of its last are output the program
produced, and a reader that removed them showed a different set of
bytes than the student saw.

Cutting exactly means naming both edges as the writer writes them.
The log begins on the line after the [[--- I/O ---]] marker, so the
opening delimiter carries the newline that ends the marker line.
It ends one blank line above [[--- exception ---]], and that blank
line belongs to the layout rather than to the log
(\cref{sec:io-section}), so the closing cut drops exactly one
newline---[[removesuffix]] rather than [[rstrip]], because a run of
newlines at the end of the section would be output too, and only the
last of them is the separator.

Anchoring both delimiters between newlines is what keeps a run's own
text from being mistaken for one: a program that prints
[[--- exception ---]] has that line recorded as [[stdout: ]] plus the
text, which the anchor does not match.
An \emph{unprefixed} line could still say it (\cref{sec:io-notes}),
and no anchor can settle that---the format has one way of spelling
both---so we take the first closing marker, as this has always done.

<<parse trailer fields>>=
io_start = block_text.find(IO_SECTION_OPEN)
if io_start >= 0:
    io_text = block_text[io_start + len(IO_SECTION_OPEN):]
    exc_start = io_text.find(EXCEPTION_SECTION_OPEN)
    if exc_start < 0:
        io_content = io_text.rstrip("\n")
    else:
        io_content = io_text[:exc_start].removesuffix("\n")
        exc_content = io_text[
            exc_start + len(EXCEPTION_SECTION_OPEN):
        ].strip()
        if exc_content and exc_content != "None":
            result["exception_info"] = exc_content
    if io_content and io_content != NO_IO_PLACEHOLDER:
        result["io_log"] = io_content
<<commits constants>>=
IO_SECTION_OPEN = f"\n{IO_SECTION_MARKER}\n"
EXCEPTION_SECTION_OPEN = f"\n{EXCEPTION_SECTION_MARKER}\n"
@

The exception section keeps its [[strip]], and the asymmetry is in the
format rather than in our attention: the I/O section has a marker
below it to cut at, while the exception text runs to the end of the
block and therefore ends in whatever the commit message ended
in---Git's closing newline included.
There is nothing there to preserve it against, so preserving it would
be preserving punctuation the recorder never wrote.

A block whose I/O section has no [[--- exception ---]] below it is not
something [[format_run_trailer]] can produce; it is what a hand-made
or truncated message looks like.
We read the section to the end of the block and drop the trailing
newlines, which is the same cut the writer would have made.

Let's verify the parser handles complete blocks, header-only
blocks (crash), and blocks with exceptions:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_parse_run_block_complete():
    from learnlog.commits import parse_run_block
    block = (
        "Run: hello.py arg1\n"
        "\n"
        "Script: hello.py\n"
        "Arguments: arg1\n"
        "Start-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:00\n"
        "End-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:05\n"
        "Exit-Code: 0\n"
        "\n"
        "--- I/O ---\n"
        "stdout: hello world\n"
        "\n"
        "--- exception ---\n"
        "None"
    )
    result = parse_run_block(block)
    assert result["script"] == "hello.py"
    assert result["arguments"] == "arg1"
    assert result["start_time"] == "2026-01-01T10:00:00"
    assert result["end_time"] == "2026-01-01T10:00:05"
    assert result["exit_code"] == "0"
    assert result["io_log"] == "stdout: hello world"
    assert result["exception_info"] is None

def test_parse_run_block_crash():
    """Header-only block indicates SIGKILL crash."""
    from learnlog.commits import parse_run_block
    block = (
        "Run: hello.py (none)\n"
        "\n"
        "Script: hello.py\n"
        "Arguments: (none)\n"
        "Start-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:00"
    )
    result = parse_run_block(block)
    assert result["script"] == "hello.py"
    assert result["end_time"] is None
    assert result["exit_code"] is None

def test_parse_run_block_exception():
    from learnlog.commits import parse_run_block
    block = (
        "Run: hello.py (none)\n"
        "\n"
        "Script: hello.py\n"
        "Arguments: (none)\n"
        "Start-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:00\n"
        "End-Time: 2026-01-01T10:00:01\n"
        "Exit-Code: 1\n"
        "\n"
        "--- I/O ---\n"
        "(no I/O)\n"
        "\n"
        "--- exception ---\n"
        "Traceback (most recent call last):\n"
        '  File "hello.py", line 1\n'
        "SyntaxError: invalid syntax"
    )
