\subsection{The line vocabulary of a captured transcript}
\label{sec:io-stream-prefixes}

The [[io_log]] field the parser hands back is a transcript, and every
line in it names the stream it came from: the capture layer writes
[[stdout: ]], [[stderr: ]] or [[stdin: ]] in front of each completed
line (\cref{capture}).
Any reader that does more than print the transcript verbatim has to
undo that---to colour input differently from output, to count the
prompts a program wrote, or to file each line as an event of its own.

We name the correspondence once, here, rather than letting each such
reader spell the three prefixes out again.
Naming it as \emph{pairs} is what makes it worth more than three bare
strings: a reader needs the stream a line belongs to as well as the
text to cut off the front of it, and taking both from one entry means
the two can never be mismatched at a call site.
The exporter's own event table is built on top of this one
(\cref{sec:chunk-io}), so the two cannot drift apart.

<<commits constants>>=
IO_STREAM_PREFIXES = (
    ("stdout: ", "stdout"),
    ("stderr: ", "stderr"),
    ("stdin: ", "stdin"),
)
@


\subsection{Extracting exception type and message}
\label{sec:parse-exception-line}

The exception section of a run block contains a Python traceback.
The last line has the exception type and message, formatted as
[[ExceptionType: message]] or just [[ExceptionType]] when there
is no message.
Splitting them apart is what lets a reader group runs by what went
wrong: the exporter fills the [[X-ExceptionType]] and
[[X-ExceptionMessage]] columns from it (\cref{progsnap2}), and the
metrics module counts syntax errors separately from runtime ones
(\cref{metrics}).

<<commits functions>>=
def parse_exception_line(exception_info):
    """Extract type and message from exception info.

    Returns ``(exception_type, exception_message)`` where
    either may be ``None``.
    """
    if not exception_info:
        return None, None

    last_line = exception_info.rstrip().split("\n")[-1]
    if ":" in last_line:
        exc_type, _, exc_msg = last_line.partition(":")
        return exc_type.strip(), exc_msg.strip()
    return last_line.strip(), None
@

Let's verify the three shapes a last line can take---a type with a
message, a type alone, and no exception section at all:

<<commits test functions>>=
def test_parse_exception_line_with_message():
    from learnlog.commits import parse_exception_line
    info = (
        "Traceback (most recent call last):\n"
        '  File "hello.py", line 1\n'
        "SyntaxError: invalid syntax"
    )
    typ, msg = parse_exception_line(info)
    assert typ == "SyntaxError"
    assert msg == "invalid syntax"

def test_parse_exception_line_no_message():
    from learnlog.commits import parse_exception_line
    info = "KeyboardInterrupt"
    typ, msg = parse_exception_line(info)
    assert typ == "KeyboardInterrupt"
    assert msg is None

def test_parse_exception_line_none():
    from learnlog.commits import parse_exception_line
    assert parse_exception_line(None) == (None, None)
    assert parse_exception_line("") == (None, None)
