\section{Filtering [[git show]] output}
\label{sec:filter-show}

We could fetch the raw commit message separately
([[git log --format=%B]]), but that would require an extra subprocess
call per commit.
Instead, we take the message out of the [[git show]] output with
[[split_commit_detail]] (\cref{sec:split-commit-detail}), rewrite it,
and put the output back together around the result.

Two different rewrites need this: merging a run's trailer back into its
header (\cref{sec:run-join}) and dropping the runs a view hides
(\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}).
Taking the rewrite as a parameter keeps the re-indent surgery in one
place instead of two.

<<commits functions>>=
def rewrite_show_message(show_output, rewrite):
    """Apply ``rewrite`` to the message inside ``git show`` output.

    ``rewrite`` maps the un-indented commit message to a new one.
    Returns the reconstructed output, or ``None`` when the
    rewritten message is empty.
    """
    header, raw_msg, diff = split_commit_detail(show_output)

    <<rewrite the commit message>>
    <<reconstruct show output>>
@

A rewrite that empties the message is a signal rather than a result:
it means every run in the commit was filtered out, so there is no
commit left to show.
Returning [[None]] lets the caller drop it entirely instead of
printing a header with nothing under it.

<<rewrite the commit message>>=
new_msg = rewrite(raw_msg)

if not new_msg.strip():
    return None
@

Re-indenting skips empty lines rather than indenting them, because
trailing whitespace on a blank line is not what [[git show]] writes,
and a reader comparing our output against git's own would see the
difference.

<<reconstruct show output>>=
reindented = []
for line in new_msg.split("\n"):
    if line:
        reindented.append("    " + line)
    else:
        reindented.append("")

return "\n".join(header + reindented + diff)
@

<<commits functions>>=
def filter_show_output(show_output, hide):
    """Filter the run blocks ``hide`` names from ``git show``.

    Returns ``None`` if all run blocks were filtered out.
    """
    return rewrite_show_message(
        show_output, lambda message: filter_runs(message, hide),
    )
@


\section{Retrieving commit detail}
\label{sec:commit-detail}

Plumbing is filtered by default because it is never part of anybody's
story---a teacher reviewing the log should not have to remember a flag
to get a clean view.
Which of the wider filters applies is a question about the
\emph{view}, not about the commit: the reading views pass their own
predicate down, chosen by [[hidden_runs]] (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}),
and this function guesses nothing about who is asking.

The trailer map is a parameter rather than something we fetch here
because callers show many commits in a row: fetching it per commit
would read the whole log once per displayed run.
Callers that have no map---tests, and anyone who only wants the commit
as it was recorded---see the header alone, with no end time or captured
I/O.

We ask for [[--no-color]] explicitly because the student's own Git
configuration reaches this command.
[[git_environment]] (\cref{gitrepo}) strips the [[GIT_*]] variables
that could redirect a write, but it deliberately keeps the config
files: that is how a clone finds its credentials.
A [[color.ui = always]] in [[~/.gitconfig]]---not a strange setting,
it is what someone puts there after being annoyed by colour
disappearing down a pipe---therefore makes Git wrap this output in
ANSI escapes even though nothing here is a terminal.
That would not merely look wrong: the diff boundary is found by
matching [[diff --git ]] at the start of a line
(\cref{sec:split-commit-detail}), and a colour code sits in front of
it, so the whole diff would be read as part of the commit message.
Asking for no colour is cheaper than teaching every reader below to
see through it.

Reordering is what a reader wants when it is shown the [[git show]]
text itself, and only then.
A caller that builds its own layout out of the three parts---the
reading view of \cref{render} composes a headline, a changes section
and a transcript---gains nothing from having them pre-shuffled, and
would have to undo the shuffle before it could tell the message from
the diff.
So [[reorder]] says which of the two the caller is: leave it alone to
get today's diff-first text, pass [[False]] to get the merged and
filtered detail in Git's own order.

<<commits functions>>=
def get_commit_detail(repo, commit_hash,
                      hide=is_internal_command,
                      trailers=None,
                      reorder=True):
    """Return ``git show`` output for a single commit,
    reordered so the diff appears before the message.

    ``trailers`` is the map from :func:`get_run_trailers`; when
    given, each run's results are merged into its header block.
    ``hide`` is the predicate on run commands whose blocks are
    stripped from the commit message: learnlog's plumbing by
    default, ``None`` for nothing (:func:`hidden_runs` picks
    the reading views' choice).  Returns ``None`` if every run
    in the commit was hidden.  With ``reorder`` false the text
    keeps git's own order, header then message then diff, for
    a caller that lays the parts out itself.
    """
    result = repo.git("show", "--no-color", commit_hash)
    detail = result.stdout

    if trailers:
        detail = rewrite_show_message(
            detail,
            lambda message: merge_run_trailers(
                message, trailers,
            ),
        )
    if hide is not None and detail is not None:
        detail = filter_show_output(detail, hide)
    if detail is None:
        return None

    return reorder_commit_detail(detail) if reorder else detail
@

