\section{Tag resolution}
\label{sec:tag-resolution}

Git tags let users mark meaningful points in a development
log---for instance, the start of each lab session.
Several readers need to know what the tags say: the ProgSnap2
exporter adds an [[X-Tag]] column to the event table and a tag
table beside it (\cref{progsnap2,sec:tag-table}), the [[play]]
command resolves tag names to starting commits
(\cref{sec:play}), and the metrics turn tags into period
boundaries (\cref{sec:metrics-tags}).

We query all tags in a single [[git for-each-ref refs/tags]]
call rather than checking each commit individually, because the
number of tags is small (typically one per lab session) and a
single subprocess call is cheaper than one per commit.
The plumbing rather than [[git tag -l]] because the porcelain
obeys the student's [[tag.sort]] configuration, so which tag
came back first depended on a setting nobody knew mattered; the
plumbing always orders by refname, which is what makes every
consumer's output deterministic.

Each tagged hash goes through [[resolve_record_commit]]
(\cref{sec:trailer-refs}) so that a tag created right after a
run finished---and therefore sitting on that run's trailer
commit---still lands on the run it marks rather than on a
commit the callers cannot find.
Resolving reads the run index (\cref{sec:run-index}), so we
read it once here and hand the same map to every tag; letting
each resolution fetch its own would walk the whole log once
per tag for one identical answer.

\subsection{A tag does not always name a commit directly}

A lightweight tag is a ref whose object \emph{is} the
commit, but [[git tag -a]] creates an intermediate
\emph{tag object} that carries the tagger, the date and a
message, and that object in turn names the commit.
Both kinds reach a learnlog repository: [[learnlog git]]
passes [[tag -a]] straight through, and so does a plain
[[git tag -a]] in a clone---which is what a teacher
marking a submission point reaches for.

The field [[%(objectname)]] reports the object the ref
points at, without dereferencing.
For an annotated tag that is the tag object's hash, and a
tag object's hash equals no commit in the log, so the
lookups below never matched and every annotated tag
vanished from [[list]], [[play]] and the ProgSnap2 export.
The peeled field [[%(*objectname)]] is the hash Git
arrives at after following the tag object, and it is
\emph{empty} for a ref that needs no following.

Neither field alone therefore covers both kinds, and
[[%(if)]] is Git's own way of choosing between them: it
emits the peeled hash when there is one and the direct
hash otherwise.
Reading both fields and choosing in Python would work
equally well, but doing it in the format string keeps
the difference between the two kinds of tag inside Git,
rather than making the loop below reason about it.

\subsection{A tag need not name a commit at all}

Peeling says which object a ref arrives at; it does not
promise that object is a commit.
A tag can name any object in the database:
[[git tag notes $(git hash-object -w notes.txt)]] tags a
blob, [[git tag base HEAD^{tree}]] tags a tree, and both
are ordinary Git that the [[learnlog git]] passthrough
forwards untouched.

Such a hash belongs to no run, and a key that is not a
commit hash is worse for this map than no key at all:
every caller looks the map up by the hash of a commit it
is displaying, so the entry can never be found, and it
sits in the map claiming otherwise.
We therefore ask Git for the object's type in the same
call---[[%(objecttype)]] and, for what a tag object peels
to, [[%(*objecttype)]]---and keep only commits.
Asking here rather than leaving the odd hashes to
[[resolve_record_commit]] costs no extra process and
keeps the two questions apart: that function decides what
a hash \emph{means}, while which hashes are worth asking
about is this map's own business.

\subsection{What an annotated tag also carries}
\label{sec:tag-details}

An annotated tag's object records a message, a tagger and a
date, and the export must carry them
(\cref{sec:tag-table})---so the reader asks for everything at
once and lets the cheaper question, which commits carry which
names, project from the answer.

[[%(contents)]] holds a trap: on a \emph{lightweight} tag it
falls through to the tagged object and reports the
\emph{commit's} message.
A reader that took the field at face value would export every
run's commit message as if it were a tag message.
The guard sits inside the format string, next to the peel
above: [[%(taggername)]] is non-empty exactly on tag objects,
so making the contents conditional on it keeps the
annotated-or-lightweight decision inside Git instead of
restating it in Python.

The fields are NUL-framed rather than space-separated because
the message is multi-line free text; a NUL is the one byte Git
never writes into a ref name, an ident or a formatted date.
The message is the only field Git cannot vouch NUL-free---a
foreign repository could hold a tag object with a NUL in its
body---so the walk trusts the declared arity until a record is
full and then requires the next fragment to look like a record
opener, gluing it back onto the message when it does not.
This is the same self-healing [[iter_log_commits]] applies to
commit messages, for the same reason, with the record opener in
the anchor role the hash line plays there.
The opener is checkable because the peeled
\enquote{hash and type} pair comes first: unlike a tag name,
it has a shape nothing else can imitate.

<<commits constants>>=
TAG_DETAIL_FORMAT = (
    "%00%(if)%(*objectname)%(then)"
    "%(*objectname) %(*objecttype)%(else)"
    "%(objectname) %(objecttype)%(end)"
    "%00%(refname:short)"
    "%00%(objecttype)"
    "%00%(taggername)"
    "%00%(taggerdate:iso-strict)"
    "%00%(if)%(taggername)%(then)%(contents)%(else)%(end)"
)
TAG_DETAIL_FIELDS = 6
TAG_OBJECT_TYPE = "tag"
COMMIT_OBJECT_TYPE = "commit"

TagDetail = collections.namedtuple(
    "TagDetail",
    ["name", "commit", "annotated", "message", "tagger", "date"],
)
<<commits functions>>=
def _tag_detail_groups(output):
    """Split `for-each-ref` output into six-field records."""
    records = []
    for fragment in output.split("\0")[1:]:
        if records and len(records[-1]) < TAG_DETAIL_FIELDS:
            records[-1].append(fragment)
        elif records and not _is_tag_record_opener(fragment):
            records[-1][-1] += "\0" + fragment
        else:
            records.append([fragment])
    return [
        record for record in records
        if len(record) == TAG_DETAIL_FIELDS
    ]
<<commits functions>>=
def _is_tag_record_opener(fragment):
    """True if `fragment` is a peeled `<hash> <type>` pair."""
    target, space, kind = fragment.partition(" ")
    return (
        space == " "
        and kind.isalpha()
        and len(target) in COMMIT_HASH_LENGTHS
        and all(
            character in "0123456789abcdef"
            for character in target
        )
    )
@

Peeling, type-filtering and trailer resolution then read exactly
as they did when the map was the product; what changed is only
that the answer keeps the annotated half instead of dropping it.
The record terminator is the newline [[for-each-ref]] appends
after the last field, so the message sheds exactly one.

<<commits functions>>=
def get_tag_details(repo):
    """Every tag that names a commit, as a `TagDetail`.

    Ordered by tag name.  `commit` is the run's header commit:
    annotated tags are reported on the commit their tag object
    names, and tags on a trailer commit are reported on the
    run's header commit instead.  `message`, `tagger` and
    `date` are empty strings for a lightweight tag;
    `annotated` tells the two kinds apart.  Tags whose object
    is not a commit are left out.
    """
    result = repo.git(
        "for-each-ref", "refs/tags",
        f"--format={TAG_DETAIL_FORMAT}",
        check=False,
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return []
    records = get_record_commits(repo)
    details = []
    for target, name, kind, tagger, date, contents in \
            _tag_detail_groups(result.stdout):
        commit_hash, _, object_type = target.partition(" ")
        if object_type != COMMIT_OBJECT_TYPE:
            continue
        details.append(TagDetail(
            name=name,
            commit=resolve_record_commit(
                repo, commit_hash, records,
            ),
            annotated=(kind == TAG_OBJECT_TYPE),
            message=contents.removesuffix("\n"),
            tagger=tagger,
            date=date,
        ))
    return details
@

Two readers of the same refs that could disagree about which
tags exist is exactly the drift the export cannot afford: the
tag table must list precisely the names the [[X-Tag]] column
mentions (\cref{sec:tag-table}).
So the map is a projection of the details rather than a second
reading.

<<commits functions>>=
def get_tag_map(repo):
    """Map commit hashes to tag names.

    Returns a dict where keys are full commit hashes
    and values are lists of tag names pointing at that
    commit.  Annotated tags are reported on the commit
    their tag object names, and tags on a trailer commit
    are reported on the run's header commit instead.
    Tags whose object is not a commit are left out.
    """
    tag_map = {}
    for tag in get_tag_details(repo):
        tag_map.setdefault(tag.commit, []).append(tag.name)
    return tag_map
@

