Those tags are all lightweight, so they exercise only the
[[%(objectname)]] half of the format string.
A test for the other half has to be careful about which
commit it tags, because [[resolve_record_commit]] hides
the bug on exactly the commit one would reach for first.
It asks Git for the tagged object's subject and for its
first parent, and both of those requests peel a tag object
on their way to a commit---so a tag object naming a
\emph{trailer} commit still comes back as that run's
header commit, by accident rather than by design.
Everywhere else, including the header commit itself, the
tag object's hash is what [[resolve_record_commit]]
returns unchanged, and the tag is lost.

So we tag a header commit by name, put one tag of each
kind on it, and assert both names against that one hash.
Dropping either half of the format string then fails the
test, and the assertion states the invariant the two kinds
share: how a tag was created must not change which commit
it lands on.

